##############################
#     DDR2 Example Files     #
##############################

This directory contains two independent DDR2 test designs. `example_top.v` and `mig_example_top.v` are alternative top-level modules and should not be used together.

## `memory.v`
A wrapper around the Xilinx MIG-generated `memory_mig` module. It exposes the MIG application interface and connects it to the physical DDR2 signals.

## `example_top.v`
A standalone top-level module for testing DDR2 with the Xilinx MIG traffic generator. 
It automatically writes test patterns, reads them back, and reports mismatches through `tg_compare_error`.
This module is intended for validating the MIG configuration and DDR2 hardware. Do not use it together with `mig_example_top.v`.

## `mem_example.v`
A custom memory-interface wrapper that converts simple read and write requests into MIG application-interface transactions. 
It supports 8-, 16-, 32-, and 64-bit accesses, byte masking, unaligned addresses, and clock-domain crossing between `boardclk` and `ui_clk`.
The module sends or receives a 128-bit DDR2 burst as two 64-bit MIG data beats.

## `mig_example_top.v`
A standalone top-level module for testing the custom memory interface. 
It generates pseudo-random addresses and data using an LFSR, writes data to DDR2, reads part of it back, compares the result, and displays the comparison result on `LED[0]`.
This is an alternative to `example_top.v` and uses custom test logic instead of the Xilinx MIG traffic generator.

## Design Relationships

```text
Xilinx automatic test:

example_top
  └─ memory
      └─ memory_mig
          └─ DDR2


Custom interface test:

mig_example_top
  └─ mem_example
      └─ memory
          └─ memory_mig
              └─ DDR2